{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966801,"nonce":"tHmZ7616Nxvwqcy6fDbxSuBNYqM=","title":"ScrollState::Scrolling can latch forever after wheel events","message":"`ScrollPhysics::tick()` returns `true` for every non-Idle state. The Scrolling state has no self-settling logic — it relies on `on_scroll_end()` (fired by `ScrollPhase::Ended`) or HIT_EDGE. Mouse-wheel inputs and many Linux trackpad drivers don't emit Ended, so without a fallback the FSM latches and pins `scroll_animating=true` → continuous redraw loop at 80-90% CPU.\n\n**Why:** discovered via cn_demo running steady at 80-90% CPU after a single scroll cycle (issue #28 follow-up). cn_demo.log showed `scroll=true` dominating 463 of 899 traced frames.\n\n**How to apply:** keep two invariants in `ScrollPhysics`:\n1. `apply_scroll_delta()` MUST stamp `last_scroll_time` itself (was relying on `apply_touch_scroll_delta`'s caller-supplied time, which the bare wheel/scrollbar-drag path skipped).\n2. `check_idle_bounce()` (called once per frame from `tick_scroll_physics`) MUST settle `Scrolling → Idle` after the idle threshold *even when not overscrolling*. The original spec only handled the overscroll-bounce case and silently ignored the non-overscrolled-quiescent case.\n\nIf you ever rename `check_idle_bounce` or split its responsibilities, keep both branches. Without them, every wheel scroll leaves a sticky `scroll_animating` redraw loop.","files":null}]}